1
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:09,660
Tell me the man, Muse —
the man of many turnings,

2
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:14,700
who was driven wandering far, once he had sacked
Troy’s holy citadel:

3
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:18,260
many were the cities of men he saw,
and he learned their minds,

4
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:21,300
many the griefs he suffered at heart
on the open sea,

5
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:25,060
striving to win his own life
and his companions’ homecoming.

6
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:28,840
Even so he could not save them,
hard though he strove:

7
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,240
they perished by their own recklessness,

8
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:34,640
fools, who ate the cattle
of Helios Hyperion,

9
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,360
and the god took from them
their day of homecoming.

10
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:41,700
Begin where you will, goddess, daughter of Zeus —
tell us, too, the tale.

11
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:44,800
By then all the others,
all who had escaped sheer ruin,

12
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,880
were home, safely past the war
and past the sea.

13
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,080
Him alone — aching for homecoming
and his wife —

14
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:55,320
the queenly nymph Calypso,
shining among goddesses, held back

15
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:58,560
in her hollow caves,
hungry to make him her husband.

16
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:01,880
But when the year came,
in the wheeling round of seasons,

17
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:05,540
in which the gods had spun the thread
of his return home to Ithaca —

18
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:09,260
not even there was he free of trials,
even among his own.

19
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:13,840
And the gods pitied him, all but Poseidon:
he raged without rest

20
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,420
against godlike Odysseus,
until the day he reached his own land.

21
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:21,660
But now Poseidon was gone
among the far-off Ethiopians —

22
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:25,960
the Ethiopians, sundered in two,
remotest of mankind,

23
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:28,940
half where Hyperion sets
and half where he rises —

24
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:34,220
to receive a hecatomb of bulls and rams,
and there he sat at the feast, delighting.

25
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:38,150
But the other gods were gathered together
in the halls of Olympian Zeus,
